# The decision journal is append-only: one JSON object per line, never edited
# in place, written concurrently by however many agent sessions are running.
# Two branches that both wrote to the same session's file therefore conflict as
# add/add or on the same trailing hunk — even though the correct answer is
# always "keep both lines", because neither side is a revision of the other.
#
# `merge=union` is git's built-in driver for exactly that shape: it takes both
# sides of a conflicting hunk instead of stopping. It does not re-sort by
# timestamp, so a merged file can be slightly out of order — that is a cosmetic
# cost against never silently dropping an entry, which is the property the
# journal exists to have.
#
# This is safe ONLY because these files are append-only. Do not extend the
# pattern to anything a writer edits in place; union-merging that would
# duplicate lines rather than reconcile them.
.coherence/**/*.jsonl merge=union
